The Supreme Court held that the High Courtâ€™s concurrent conviction of the husband under **ss.â€¯498A** (cruelty) and **306** (abetment of suicide) of the IPC could not be sustained unless the ingredients of **abetment of suicide** were independently proved.  

- **Cruelty (s.â€¯498A)** is punishable under **sectionâ€¯85** of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, which prescribes imprisonment of up to three years and a fine for subjecting a married woman to crueltyâ€¯[cdd566a9-9cf8-41ac-8b0e-e8549d1eefef].  
- **Abetment of suicide (s.â€¯306)** attracts the presumption under **sectionâ€¯117**: the Court may presume abetment only when it is shown that the woman committed suicide **within seven years of marriage** and that her husband (or his relative) had subjected her to crueltyâ€¯[a2e1dff3-b578-4b45-9679-a33775d91a58].  

The Court observed that mere proof of cruelty (s.â€¯498A) does **not** automatically satisfy the requirements for s.â€¯306; the presumption of abetment arises only if the suicide occurred within the stipulated sevenâ€‘year period and the cruelty is linked to the suicide. In the absence of such proof, the High Court erred in convicting the husband under both sections. Consequently, the Supreme Court set aside the conviction under s.â€¯306 (abetment of suicide) while allowing the conviction under s.â€¯498A (cruelty) to stand, provided the cruelty was independently established.  

Thus, the Supreme Court resolved the issue by limiting liability under s.â€¯306 to cases where the statutory presumption (or direct evidence) of abetment is attracted, and it held that the High Court was not justified in imposing a concurrent sentence for both offences without satisfying those conditions.
